hard  helms  and cynthia told me and these are the  moments in the book that i love that  where i  really try to humanize the directors  helms would come home at night and tell  her that he had lash marks  on his back not literally but  figuratively because bobby kennedy had  hectored him  all day long about getting rid of fidel  castro  by fair means or foul the kennedys were  obsessed  with that and the other thing that she  told me that i found just  kind of poignant well as you know chris  she said  they were all asked to do things they  shouldn't have done  and god knows helms was a flawed  character  he did cross the line and he committed  illegal acts for lbj  mh chaos mk ultra all kinds of things  and of course at the end of the day when  the crunch came he stood up to richard  nixon and refused to participate  in the watergate cover-up  and chris you make a good point i think  throughout the book that whatever the ca  has done and their failures all along  their career their history of the cia  it's usually done at the behest of the  president right  that's right and one former director  told me brian that there will always be  a cia you could never abolish it because  presidents would then have no one to  blame there's a famous lament  at langley and it is in this town there  are only  policy successes and intelligence  failures  and of course the cia was blamed for  uh 9 11 but i have chapter and verse in  my chapter  on the walk up to 9 11 in which george  tennant and his lieutenants  went over to the white house pounded the  table and told condi rice we had to go  on a war footing now  this was in july of 2001 and yet they  were blamed which is not to say that the  cia hasn't had plenty of screw-ups  and debacles uh over the years but  usually  it's because the president asked them to  do something  all right well chris sipple thank you  very much this is a great book i really  enjoyed it it's a terrific read once  again terrific work for a former  colleague  chris whipple thank you so much brian  great to see you  coming up the winners and losers this  week in the media as  chosen by the editors at mediaite tucker  carlson once again  on the list when we come back you're  watching the law and crime trial network  continuing now with this week's winners  and losers in the media as chosen by the  editors at mediaite and we're joined by  aida mclaughlin who's the  editor-in-chief of mediaite  which like the law and crime trial  network is owned by the dan  abrams media empire aidan thank you so  much for joining us again today  and tonight's first loser is tucker  carlson  once again on your list  yeah if you're the mayor he's  pulled that story off its website the  station is no longer reporting that the  mayor's office covered up the  coronavirus numbers it's now  been reported that some of those numbers  were actually put out in august so the  public didn't seem to know about them  the mayor's office claims emails  suggesting a cover-up were taken out of  context  so we don't know the truth about that  because we don't have all the emails but  we wanted to keep you up to date on what  was happening there and of course we'll  bring you more  so aidan there's a story he's reporting  based on a local station which  completely retracted the story  and yet tucker carlson doesn't seem to  want to buy that  right so let me just give you a little  context on this last thursday tucker  carlson covered this local nashville  story  which had alleged that the national  mayor's office was covering up good news  about the coronavirus  about its spread in bars and restaurants  and tucker crossing covered this at the  beginning of his show and covered it  pretty  extensively and cited the story as  conclusive proof  that leaders particularly democratic  ones were lying to people  about good news about the coronavirus  because for they somehow wanted to keep  power over their population  now i watched this this tucker crossing  segment on thursday night and it didn't  make a lot of sense because  obviously mayors don't want bars and  restaurants in their cities to be closed  for no reason  uh you know they want their cities to  have functioning economies  but anyway the the story that tucker  carlson was basing  uh his commentary on ended up getting  debunked and retracted by  fox 19 the outlet that first reported it  and  it got you know fox and friends which  also covered the story on monday  apologized for covering it uh tucker  carlson comes on his show and instead of  apologizing or correcting  says you know it presents it as an  update says that it was  retracted because of pressure from the  mayor's office which doesn't really make  any sense because  the mayor's office is putting pressure  on the outlet to begin with uh so it's  unclear  why now they would be retracting the  story uh and basically said there's  still questions about whether or not  there's a cover-up  he's now alleging a cover-up about the  cover-up so it was a sort of  pretty embarrassing display from tucker  carlson and i i think genuinely  misleading his viewers about  what this report said and about what it  was what the  outlet was retracting  absolutely and as we all know aidan uh  journalism 101 the most important rule  is if you make a mistake and everyone  does  retract it immediately set the record  straight he doesn't seem to want to do  that  if it cuts across his theory of what's  going on  exactly it's kind of a really disturbing  place  for uh a jurassic enterprise to bri to  be  you know you can have your concerns  about fox news but it's a news outlet  and typically they're good at correcting  things that they get wrong  it's really not a good sign that  when a story has been debunked and  retracted completely  the tucker carlson is misleading his  viewers about that and refusing to  correct it himself  let's go to the winner ali veshi of  msnbc who was hit by a rubber bullet  covering up police protest  and yet the president of the united  states thought that was a wonderful  thing  listen to this all right guys i got hit  yeah i got hit hold on  i remember this guy welchie he got hit  on the knee with a  canister of tear gas and he went down  he didn't he was down my knee  my knee nobody cared these guys didn't  care they moved them aside  and they just walked right it was like  it was the most beautiful thing  a wonderful thing apparently from the  president's point of view  yeah i mean i think we can all agree  that that saying a  anyone getting hit by a rubber bullet uh  calling it a wonderful thing is really  bad  uh particularly for the president to be  doing about a member of the press  uh ali velshi he president trump  mispronounced his name there  but his name is ali velshi he's a host  at msnbc and for the past  year he's really been out in the field a  lot covering the protests  against police brutality uh covering the  coronavirus and  uh a series of other things across  america and  while he was covering these protests  against police brutality he got shot by  a rubber bullet  um it was a pretty staggering moment and  a sign of what reporters have to go  through in the field and it's  embarrassing that  the president is celebrating it  and alive one of the hardest working  people certainly at msnbc or on tv  at all aid mclaughlin thank you so much  for being here  thanks to all of you for watching and  thanks again to the team at long crime  for getting us  on the air and getting us off the air  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

